$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395799 1072 #uuid "6acd5ad6-cecd-50bc-9edc-fc2a85e9b7ff" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395799 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =5(0.5)^{4}-(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.062\\,5-1\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =5(0.25)^{4}-(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.003\\,90...-1\\cdot 0.000\\,97... \\\\  & =0.018\\,55... \\\\  & ≈0.019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.018\\,55....$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.018\\,55...}=15.157\\,89…≈15.158.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈15.157\\,89… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.157\\,89…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.922… \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order fro