rder describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395850 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395851 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395850 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395852 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395850 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395853 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395850 