5937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395938 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395943 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395947 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1069 "q-334829" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1072 #uuid "70e96342-9f6f-5f4d-885c-91889dc5a387" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395937 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n